# Dev / Test Servers (lanpub, HTTPS, LAN)

Whenever the user says "start the dev server" or "start the test server":
never hand them a raw `host:port`. Serve everything through `lanpub` and give
the `https://<app>.local/…` address, reachable from this machine and every
device on the LAN/tailnet. Prefer the same single-host model as prod: UI + API
on ONE host — no separate API hostname. The app's routes (`/`, `/settings`, …)
work identically in dev and prod.

## One-time setup (already done on this box)

- `lanproxy.service` is installed and enabled: a small Go daemon that serves
  HTTPS on :443, issues per-host certs from a local CA, and publishes each
  route's name over mDNS. Nothing to start per project.
- Trust for other devices: run `lanpub trust` on the server, then install the
  CA path it prints on the device (Android: Settings → Security → Encryption &
  credentials → Install a certificate → CA certificate; iOS: install profile +
  enable full trust). Browsers warn until then.

## Start (exact command)

```bash
lanpub <app> <dev-server-port>    # e.g. lanpub advui 5173
```

That writes the route and prints the URL. It is live on the next request — no
proxy restart, no reload, nothing to stop afterwards. Routes persist across
restarts, so re-run it only when the port changes.

Use `lanpub --list` to see what is routed, `lanpub --remove <app>` to drop one,
`lanpub --doctor` to diagnose a broken setup.

Point the name at the **dev-server** (e.g. Vite) port, not the backend: the dev
server proxies `/api` + `/ws` to the backend, and prod's server serves UI + API
on one port too, so the test URL mirrors prod exactly.

## Canonical dev/test names (mirror prod: one host)

- UI = API host: `https://<app>.local/` plus the app's own routes —
  exactly like prod's single origin.
- No separate `<app>api.local`: the dev server proxies `/api` + `/ws` to
  the backend.
- Names live in `~/.lanproxy/routes` and survive proxy restarts; re-run
  `lanpub <app> <port>` whenever the underlying port changes.

## SPA / client-side routes

- Deep links and refresh must serve `index.html` with a 200. If the
  backend uses a static-file `not_found_service` fallback it may return
  the index body but keep a 404 status — fine for browsers, but `curl`
  checks should expect 200 only on explicitly registered routes.

## Gotchas (learned the hard way)

- Vite 5 has no `--allowedHosts` CLI flag (v6+ does). For `.local`
  hostnames add `allowedHosts: true` under `server:` in the Vite config
  (temp test edit — revert before commit).
- Dev-server `server.proxy` `/api` + `/ws` targets must point at the
  *backend* port in use; fix before starting it or API calls 404/stall.
- No dedicated `/health` route is common — a 404 there often means the
  server *is* answering. Health-check against a real API endpoint instead.
- Orca's embedded browser (Electron) does not trust the local CA even
  after `~/.pki/nssdb` install → blank page. Verify via `curl -sk` and
  the user's own system-Chromium browser instead.
- `.localhost` names resolve only on this machine; `.local` needs avahi
  on the server side and mDNS working on the client (Android is flaky —
  fall back to `https://<server-host>:<port>` via MagicDNS).
- Every route gets its **own** certificate with an exact SAN, because
  OpenSSL 3.6+ (curl, python) refuses a `*.local` wildcard — the wildcard's
  base is a single label. `openssl s_client -connect <app>.local:443
  -servername <app>.local` verifies the chain.
- A 502 with "nothing is listening on 127.0.0.1:<port>" means the dev
  server is not running yet, not that the route is wrong. `lanpub --list`
  shows the port each name points at.
